WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.222
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> <b>Is too much choice making us unhappy?</b>

00:00:05.523 --> 00:00:08.747
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Buying jeans isn’t as easy as it used to be.

00:00:09.445 --> 00:00:13.836
Years ago, there was only one kind of jeans – probably Levis.

00:00:14.788 --> 00:00:22.361
Nowadays, there are hundreds – different styles, different colours, different lengths, with buttons, with zips.

00:00:23.336 --> 00:00:28.464
There are so many options that you feel the perfect pair must be waiting for you somewhere…

00:00:29.845 --> 00:00:36.471
And it isn’t just jeans. In big supermarkets we have to choose between thousands of products –

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:41.154
my local supermarket has 35 different kinds of milk!

00:00:42.796 --> 00:00:48.279
When we’re buying clothes or electrical gadgets, ordering a coffee in a café,

00:00:48.946 --> 00:00:53.779
looking for a hotel on a travel website, deciding which TV channel to watch,

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:57.884
or even choosing a future partner on a dating website,

00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:01.820
 we constantly have to choose from hundreds of possibilities.

00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:07.962
People often think that being able to choose from a lot of options is a good thing.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:17.068
However, university researchers have discovered that too much choice is making us feel unhappy and dissatisfied.

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:25.162
The problem is that we have so many options that we get stressed every time we have to make a decision

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:28.136
because we’re worried about making the wrong one.

00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:35.014
Then when we choose one thing, we feel bad because we think we are missing other opportunities,

00:01:35.704 --> 00:01:38.679
and this makes us dissatisfied with what we’ve chosen.

00:01:40.178 --> 00:01:45.112
Research also shows that we feel happier when we have less choice.

00:01:45.842 --> 00:01:49.407
In a study, Professor Mark Lepper at Stanford University

00:01:49.462 --> 00:01:53.621
found that people who tried six kinds of jam  and then chose one

00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:58.906
 felt happier with their choice than those who were offered 24 jams to taste.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.371
But if all this choice is bad for us, what can we do about it?

00:02:05.546 --> 00:02:10.749
Professor Lepper suggests that we should try to relax when we have to decide what to buy.

00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:16.453
‘Don’t take these choices too seriously or it will become stressful,’ he says.

00:02:17.564 --> 00:02:20.868
‘If you pick a sofa from IKEA in 30 seconds,

00:02:20.947 --> 00:02:26.557
 you’ll feel better than if you spend hours researching sofas – because you won’t know what you’re missing.’